  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the file set covering both sides of the trace:\n\n**Netty pipeline exception propagation:**\n- `AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 core `exceptionCaught` propagation/invocation logic\n- `DefaultChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 pipeline-level exception routing, tail's default logging behavior when nothing handles it\n- `ChannelPipeline.java`, `ChannelHandlerContext.java`, `ChannelInboundHandler.java`, `ChannelHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 interfaces/defaults `exceptionCaught` relies on\n- `ChannelInitializer.java`, `CombinedChannelDuplexHandler.java` \u2014 additional exceptionCaught call sites\n\n**Spring WebFlux \u2194 reactor-netty boundary:**\n- `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 the actual adapter wrapping reactor-netty's `HttpServer`/`HttpServerRequest`/`HttpServerResponse` into Spring's reactive server API (this is where the Netty exception has to cross into Spring's model)\n- `ReactorServerHttpRequest.java`, `ReactorServerHttpResponse.java`, `AbstractServerHttpResponse.java` \u2014 request/response wrapping\n- `HttpWebHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 top-level `HttpHandler` that owns request handling and last-resort error handling before the response is committed\n- `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler.java`, `WebExceptionHandler.java`, `ResponseStatusExceptionHandler.java`, `WebHandler.java` \u2014 Spring's own exception-handling chain, where original Netty-level context (channel, cause chain) is typically discarded down to just an HTTP status\n- `WebHttpHandlerBuilder.java` \u2014 wires the exception-handling decorator chain together\n- `ReactorHttpServer.java` (test fixture) \u2014 shows the reactor-netty server bootstrap wiring used to bridge the two stacks\n\nThis set is sufficient to trace: Netty handler throws \u2192 `exceptionCaught` propagates through the pipeline \u2192 reactor-netty's own pipeline handler converts it into a reactive error signal \u2192 `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter` surfaces it as a `Mono` error \u2192 `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler`/`ResponseStatusExceptionHandler` catches it and collapses it to a generic HTTP status, which is the point where the original Netty-level cause/context is lost.",
